How a page gets rendered

WordPress's normal template hierarchy is only partly used here:

Static pages (page-{slug}.php) — standard WordPress filename convention. WordPress automatically uses page-billboard.php for a page with the slug billboard, etc. No extra registration needed.
Events (the event custom post type) — do NOT use WordPress's usual single-event.php naming convention. Instead, a single_template filter in functions.php explicitly routes every event post to main_display.php, regardless of that file's name. This was a deliberate choice — the display template is written to be content-type-agnostic in the long run, not permanently tied to being "the event template" by filename.

Every wpos page/template calls oswp_forty_start() at the top and oswp_forty_end() at the bottom (both in functions.php, named _forty_ on disk for historical reasons — they are wpos's shell functions) — these print the shared header/nav/footer HTML. A small number of pages not yet migrated to wpos still use the older oswp_page_start() / oswp_page_end() pair, which wraps Twenty Twenty-Five's block-theme template parts instead.

How content data works
Events are entered entirely through ACF fields (the block editor/WYSIWYG is explicitly disabled for this post type — see remove_post_type_support('event', 'editor') in functions.php).
oswp_get_event_record($post_id) in functions.php is the single source of truth for reading an event's full data — merges every ACF field with the post's taxonomy term assignments (staff/venue/event_category) into one flat array.
Display templates are written to be reductive: they check whether a given field has a value and only render that section if it does. No field is ever required to exist for a page to render correctly. This applies throughout — a missing featured image, missing tile images, missing summary, etc. all silently produce a shorter page, never an error.
ACF's Location Rules and Conditional Logic control what Authors see while editing a post — this is entirely separate from what's shown on the public page. A field can be hidden from the editing screen (e.g. movie-specific fields, only shown when the event's category is Film) while still rendering publicly if it happens to have a saved value, and vice versa.
wpos's foundation: Forty (required files and dependencies)

wpos's visual language — layout grid, typography, colour palette, components (buttons, boxes, tiles) — comes from Forty, vendored (copied, not npm-installed) directly into assets/. File names on disk still say "Forty"/"main" (main.css, main.js) rather than "wpos" — this is a naming/branding decision only, not yet applied to the actual files. Source: HTML5UP's Forty template, originally obtained via a Jekyll build at ~/sites/karamu/pasupe/assets/ — if any of these files are ever missing, re-copy from there rather than downloading fresh (this exact recovery happened once already).

Required files, and why each exists:

assets/css/
├── main.css              Forty's own stylesheet. @imports
│                          font-awesome.min.css by relative path —
│                          that file MUST sit in this same directory
│                          or the icon font silently fails to load.
├── font-awesome.min.css  Icon font CSS, imported by main.css.
└── custom.css            Site-specific overrides. Must be enqueued
                           at a HIGHER priority number than main.css
                           (higher number = loads/prints later =
                           wins the CSS cascade). Currently: main.css
                           at 21, custom.css at 22. Get this backwards
                           and overrides silently do nothing.

assets/fonts/              FontAwesome's actual font files (.woff,
                            .woff2, .ttf, .eot, .svg, .otf) —
                            referenced by font-awesome.min.css.

assets/js/                 Must be enqueued in this exact dependency
├── jquery                 order (see wp_enqueue_script calls in
├── skel.min.js             functions.php) — main.js calls both skel
├── jquery.scrolly.min.js   and $(...).scrolly() directly on
├── util.js                 document ready, so both MUST load before
└── main.js                 main.js or it throws a JS error and the
                             rest of the script (including the menu
                             toggle) never runs. jquery.scrollex.min.js
                             is deliberately NOT included — confirmed
                             unused; only add it if a console error
                             specifically names it.

assets/images/              Forty's own placeholder/demo images
                             (pic01.jpg etc.) plus the site's actual
                             uploaded images. banner.jpg specifically
                             is referenced directly in main.css's
                             #banner rule (background-image) — if
                             replacing the homepage banner image,
                             either overwrite this exact filename or
                             override the rule in custom.css.

Enqueue order (all in functions.php, all as wp_enqueue_style/ wp_enqueue_script calls hooked to wp_enqueue_scripts):

main.css (priority 21)
custom.css (priority 22 — must be higher/later than main.css)
jquery (WordPress's own bundled copy, not Forty's jquery.min.js — avoids loading two copies)
skel.min.js
jquery.scrolly.min.js
util.js
main.js (must be last — depends on all of the above)

If the menu toggle stops working or the console shows skel is not defined / $(...).scrolly is not a function, this dependency chain is the first thing to check.

Design language

wpos follows Forty's own existing visual conventions wherever possible — font sizes, colour palette, spacing, border treatment — rather than introducing new ad-hoc styling. Where Forty has no existing pattern for something (e.g. image captions), new CSS is composed from Forty's existing values (same font-size steps, same muted-colour convention) rather than styled arbitrarily. The goal is for wpos to read as one coherent system, not a base template with bolted-on exceptions.

Known limitations / deliberate simplifications
No ACF Pro — Gallery and Repeater field types are unavailable. Where a "several of these" input is needed (e.g. multiple tile images), the workaround is individually numbered fields (big_tile_image_1, big_tile_image_2, etc.) rather than a single repeating field.
Image cropping for tile display currently uses a CSS-only trick (object-fit: cover + aspect-ratio) for visual squaring, not a genuine server-side crop pipeline tied to the registered WordPress image sizes — this is a known gap, not a bug.
No payment/checkout system is built yet — ticket/booking links are currently plain URLs entered per-event via ACF. A shared, event-parameterized checkout flow (handing off to Stripe, never storing card data in WordPress) is planned but not implemented.
Contributing / editing conventions
Never edit wp-content/themes/twentytwentyfive/ — it's the parent theme, kept stock.
All real work happens in wp-content/themes/oswp-child/.
Check Forty's existing CSS/markup conventions before adding new styling — reuse before inventing.
Run php -l on any PHP file after editing, before assuming it works.
Commit reasonably often — large uncommitted local changes have been lost before during branch operations.
